From 6fcdd2de66307406ce8b0b05e70fcf04aba868aa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 02:49:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Player information: the dialogue normalisation by name and
 unit, and what passthrough sends

The overview page had one row, "Dialnorm", for two different things: the
dialnorm of AC-3 and E-AC-3 is the level of the dialogue below full scale
(-27 dBFS: a receiver lowers the sound by 4 dB), the DIALNORM of DTS is the
dialogue normalisation gain that a decoder applies (-10 dB: it lowers the sound
by 10 dB). Both showed as "-27 dB" and "-10 dB".

- AudioPresentation.h gives a Dialnorm: its kind (a level or a gain) and its
  value, with its text in its unit ("-27 dBFS", "-10 dB").
- The overview page has a row for each: "Dialogue level" for AC-3 and E-AC-3,
  "Dialogue gain" for DTS. With "Dialogue normalisation at 0 dB in passthrough",
  each says what is sent in its place: "-27 dBFS (sent as -31 dBFS)",
  "-10 dB (sent as 0 dB)". The passthrough codec tells the data cache what it
  sends (CStream::Sent()); nothing while it sends what the stream says.
- New labels Player.Process(audiodialoguelevel), audiodialnormgain and
  audiodialnormsent; Player.Process(audio.dialnorm), named as in the CoreELEC
  builds, keeps what the stream says, now with its unit.
- system/hades made again with make-playerinfo.py.

Estuary: #32244 is now "Dialogue level"; #32364 "Dialogue gain", #32365
"sent as", in en_gb, es_es, es_ar and es_mx.
